from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["hezekiah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Hezekiah was", """Hezekiah was king of Judah, the southern kingdom, from about 715 to 686 BC. He became king at 25. His father, Ahaz, was one of Judah’s worst kings: he worshiped foreign gods, shut the doors of the Temple, and even sacrificed his own son in a fire. Hezekiah grew up in a palace where that was normal.

The Bible says that Hezekiah “trusted in the Lord, the God of Israel, so that there was none like him among all the kings of Judah after him or among those who were before him.”""", ("2 Kings", 18)),
("Why his story matters", """Hezekiah’s story has three big scenes: a spiritual revival, a deadly enemy at the gates, and a death sentence that was turned back. It shows what a leader can do when he turns to God, and also how one proud moment can cost a lot.""")],
cards=[
C("Reopening the Temple", "In his first month as king, Hezekiah reopened the Temple, cleaned it out and led the nation back to worship.",
"""In the first year of his reign, in the first month, Hezekiah opened the doors of the Temple and repaired them. He gathered the priests and Levites and said, “Listen to me, Levites! Consecrate yourselves, and consecrate the house of the Lord. Our fathers have been unfaithful and have done what was evil.” It took sixteen days to carry the filth out of the Temple and to purify it.

Then the king led a great service of worship. He invited the whole nation, including the northern tribes, to come to Jerusalem for the Passover, which had not been kept properly for years. Messengers went all over the land. Some people laughed at them, but many came. The Bible says, “There was great joy in Jerusalem, for since the time of Solomon the son of David there had been nothing like this.”

Hezekiah also broke the bronze snake that Moses had made in the wilderness. Over the centuries, people had started burning incense to it as an idol. He called it Nehushtan, which means “a piece of bronze.”""", ("2 Chronicles", 29)),
C("The Assyrian army", "In 701 BC the Assyrian king Sennacherib invaded Judah and surrounded Jerusalem.",
"""Assyria was the superpower of the time and had already destroyed the northern kingdom of Israel. Hezekiah refused to keep paying tribute to Assyria. In 701 BC, Sennacherib invaded and captured 46 walled cities of Judah. Assyrian records, which still survive, brag that he shut Hezekiah up “like a bird in a cage” in Jerusalem.

Hezekiah prepared. He blocked the springs outside the city, and he had workers dig a tunnel about 1,750 feet long through solid rock to bring water from the Gihon spring inside the walls. The tunnel can still be walked through in Jerusalem today. He also repaired the wall and gathered the people. “Be strong and courageous. Do not be afraid or dismayed before the king of Assyria... With him is an arm of flesh, but with us is the Lord our God, to help us and to fight our battles.”

Sennacherib’s spokesman stood outside the wall and shouted in Hebrew so everyone on the wall could hear: Don’t let Hezekiah deceive you. No god has ever saved his people from Assyria. Why would yours?""", ("2 Kings", 18)),
C("A letter spread before the Lord", "Hezekiah took the Assyrian threat to the Temple and prayed. That night the Assyrian army was struck down.",
"""Sennacherib sent a letter of threat. Hezekiah read it, went into the Temple and spread it out before the Lord. He prayed: “O Lord, the God of Israel, enthroned above the cherubim, you are the God, you alone, of all the kingdoms of the earth... Incline your ear, O Lord, and hear; open your eyes, O Lord, and see; and hear all the words of Sennacherib... So now, O Lord our God, save us from his hand, that all the kingdoms of the earth may know that you, O Lord, are God alone.”

The prophet Isaiah sent word that the Lord had heard. Sennacherib would not enter the city, not even shoot an arrow into it. That night the angel of the Lord struck down 185,000 men in the Assyrian camp. When the people of Jerusalem woke up, the dead were everywhere. Sennacherib left and went home to Nineveh. Years later, in 681 BC, his own sons killed him while he was worshiping in a temple, just as Isaiah had said.""", ("2 Kings", 19)),
C("Fifteen more years", "Hezekiah became deathly ill. Isaiah said he would die, but Hezekiah prayed, and God added fifteen years to his life.",
"""Around that time Hezekiah got sick and was near death. Isaiah came to him with a message: “Set your house in order, for you shall die; you shall not recover.” Hezekiah turned his face to the wall and prayed, weeping bitterly: “Remember now, O Lord, how I have walked before you in faithfulness and with a whole heart, and have done what is good in your sight.”

Before Isaiah had left the courtyard, God sent him back: “I have heard your prayer; I have seen your tears. Behold, I will heal you. On the third day you shall go up to the house of the Lord. And I will add fifteen years to your life.” A poultice of figs was put on the boil. As a sign, God made the shadow on the stairway go back ten steps.

Hezekiah had no son yet. His son Manasseh was born during those added years, and he was twelve when he became king. The Bible says Manasseh turned out to be as bad as Ahaz or worse. God’s gift of life did not guarantee what came after.""", ("2 Kings", 20)),
C("The visitors from Babylon", "Hezekiah proudly showed Babylonian envoys all his treasures. Isaiah said Babylon would one day carry it all away.",
"""After Hezekiah’s recovery, messengers came from Babylon, a rising power then still small, with letters and a gift. Hezekiah was pleased. He showed them everything: the silver, the gold, the spices, the armory and every treasure in his storehouses. “There was nothing in his house or in all his realm that Hezekiah did not show them.”

Isaiah asked, “What did these men say? Where did they come from? What have they seen in your house?” Then he gave a message from God: “Behold, the days are coming, when all that is in your house, and that which your fathers have stored up till this day, shall be carried to Babylon. Nothing shall be left.” Some of Hezekiah’s own descendants would become servants in the palace of the king of Babylon.

Hezekiah’s answer was, “The word of the Lord that you have spoken is good. For there will be peace and security in my days.” It was a sad moment for a great king: he was relieved that the disaster would not come in his own lifetime. About a hundred years later it came true.""", ("2 Kings", 20))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Hezekiah’s best moment was a simple one: he took a threatening letter into the Temple and spread it out before God. When something seems too big, that is a pattern we can follow. Bring it to the Lord, and say it as it is.

His story also shows that prayer changes things. Hezekiah was told that he would die and he prayed. The Bible says God heard.

But it also warns us about success. After the victory and the healing, Hezekiah got proud and showed off his treasures. How we handle blessing matters as much as how we handle trouble. Staying humble and thinking about the generations after us is part of a faithful life.""", ("2 Kings", 19))])

LONG["deborah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Deborah was", """Deborah lived about 1200 BC, in the time of the judges. This was a period when Israel kept turning away from God, being attacked by neighbors, crying out for help, and being rescued by a leader God raised up. Deborah was one of those leaders, and the only woman among the judges.

The Bible calls her a prophetess, the wife of Lappidoth. She held court under a palm tree between Ramah and Bethel, in the hill country of Ephraim, and Israelites came to her to settle their disputes.""", ("Judges", 4)),
("Why her story matters", """Deborah’s story is about faith, leadership and teamwork. It shows that God uses people of any background, and that the real victory belongs to the Lord.""")],
cards=[
C("Twenty years of oppression", "A Canaanite king named Jabin, with his commander Sisera and 900 iron chariots, oppressed Israel for twenty years.",
"""Israel had done evil again, and God let them fall into the hands of Jabin, king of Canaan, who ruled from the city of Hazor in the north. His army commander was Sisera, and he had 900 chariots fitted with iron. For the Israelite farmers, who fought on foot with simple weapons, that was a terrifying force, especially on flat ground.

“And he oppressed the people of Israel cruelly for twenty years.” Finally the people cried out to the Lord for help. The roads were unsafe, and villagers stopped traveling. God’s answer came through a woman sitting under a tree.""", ("Judges", 4)),
C("The call to Barak", "Deborah summoned Barak and told him God said to gather 10,000 men at Mount Tabor.",
"""Deborah sent for a man named Barak, from the tribe of Naphtali. She said to him: “Has not the Lord, the God of Israel, commanded you, ‘Go, gather your men at Mount Tabor, taking 10,000 from the people of Naphtali and Zebulun. And I will draw out Sisera, the general of Jabin’s army, to meet you by the river Kishon with his chariots and his troops, and I will give him into your hand’?”

Barak answered, “If you will go with me, I will go, but if you will not go with me, I will not go.” It was either caution or a wish for Deborah’s support. Deborah said: “I will surely go with you. Nevertheless, the road on which you are going will not lead to your glory, for the Lord will sell Sisera into the hand of a woman.” She went with him, and the army gathered.""", ("Judges", 4)),
C("The battle at the Kishon", "When Sisera’s chariots attacked, Deborah said, “Up! The Lord has given Sisera into your hand.” The Lord threw the enemy into confusion.",
"""Sisera heard that Barak was at Mount Tabor, and he brought all 900 chariots and his army to the Kishon River in the valley below. Deborah said to Barak, “Up! For this is the day in which the Lord has given Sisera into your hand. Does not the Lord go out before you?”

Barak led the 10,000 men down the mountain. “And the Lord routed Sisera and all his chariots and all his army before Barak by the edge of the sword.” Deborah’s victory song later says that heavy rain flooded the Kishon, “the torrent Kishon swept them away,” which would have turned the plain to mud and trapped chariots. The great strength of Sisera’s army became a trap. Sisera jumped down from his chariot and fled on foot. The whole army was killed.""", ("Judges", 4)),
C("Jael and the tent peg", "Sisera ran to the tent of Jael. While he slept, she killed him with a tent peg, as Deborah had foretold.",
"""Sisera ran to the tent of a woman named Jael, wife of Heber the Kenite. Heber’s family was at peace with King Jabin, so Sisera thought he would be safe. Jael welcomed him: “Turn aside, my lord; turn aside to me; do not be afraid.” She covered him with a rug, gave him milk when he asked for water, and promised to say no one was there.

Exhausted, Sisera fell asleep. Jael took a tent peg and a hammer, went quietly to him and drove the peg through his head into the ground. When Barak arrived looking for Sisera, Jael went out to meet him: “Come, and I will show you the man whom you are seeking.” He was lying dead with the tent peg in his temple. Just as Deborah had said, the honor of killing Sisera went to a woman. Soon after, the Israelites destroyed King Jabin as well.""", ("Judges", 4)),
C("The song of Deborah", "Deborah and Barak sang a victory song, giving praise to God. The land had peace for forty years.",
"""Deborah and Barak then sang a long song that is one of the oldest poems in the Bible. It begins, “That the leaders took the lead in Israel, that the people offered themselves willingly, bless the Lord!” It praises the tribes who came to fight and mentions those who stayed home. It describes how the stars and the river fought against Sisera. And it praises Jael.

Deborah also describes herself in the song: “Until I arose; I, Deborah, arose as a mother in Israel.” The song ends with a prayer: “So may all your enemies perish, O Lord! But your friends be like the sun as he rises in his might.” The Bible then says, “And the land had rest for forty years.”""", ("Judges", 5))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Deborah shows that God does not look for the person that culture expects. She had no army, no throne, no title beyond the judge’s seat under a palm tree. But she listened to God, spoke clearly and led with courage. Her faith gave Barak courage, too.

Her story also shows teamwork. Deborah, Barak, Jael and 10,000 ordinary soldiers each had a part. Nobody did it alone. The Bible’s praise goes to “those who offered themselves willingly.”

And note who gets the credit: the Lord. The song says, “To the Lord I will sing; I will make melody to the Lord, the God of Israel.” When we are faced with a force that looks too big, the question is not whether we have the iron chariots. It is whether God is going out before us.""", ("Judges", 5))])

LONG["isaiah"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Isaiah was", """Isaiah, the son of Amoz, was a prophet in Jerusalem from about 740 to 680 BC. He served under four kings of Judah: Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah. He lived in a dangerous time. The mighty Assyrian empire was swallowing the countries around Judah, and the northern kingdom of Israel was destroyed in 722 BC. Isaiah often spoke directly to kings, and he seems to have been well connected in the capital.

His name means “the Lord saves.” The book of Isaiah has 66 chapters and is one of the most quoted books in the New Testament.""", ("Isaiah", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Isaiah gave some of the most famous promises in the Bible, including the promise of a child who would be called “God with us” and a servant who would suffer for others. Jesus Himself read from Isaiah when He began His public ministry.""")],
cards=[
C("The year King Uzziah died", "Isaiah saw the Lord on a throne, high and lifted up, and heard angels calling, “Holy, holy, holy.”",
"""In 740 BC, King Uzziah died. He had been a good king for 52 years, and the country felt shaken. In that year, Isaiah went into the Temple and saw a vision. “I saw the Lord sitting upon a throne, high and lifted up; and the train of his robe filled the temple.” Above him stood seraphim, angelic beings with six wings, calling out to each other: “Holy, holy, holy is the Lord of hosts; the whole earth is full of his glory!”

The doorposts shook and the house filled with smoke. Isaiah’s reaction was not excitement but fear: “Woe is me! For I am lost; for I am a man of unclean lips, and I dwell among a people of unclean lips; for my eyes have seen the King, the Lord of hosts!”""", ("Isaiah", 6)),
C("“Here I am. Send me.”", "A seraph touched Isaiah’s lips with a burning coal. When God asked who would go, Isaiah said, “Here I am! Send me.”",
"""One of the seraphim flew to Isaiah with a burning coal taken from the altar. He touched Isaiah’s mouth with it and said, “Behold, this has touched your lips; your guilt is taken away, and your sin atoned for.” God had met Isaiah’s confession with cleansing.

Then Isaiah heard the Lord say, “Whom shall I send, and who will go for us?” And he answered, “Here I am! Send me.” God’s commission was hard. He told Isaiah that the people would hear and not understand. Isaiah asked how long, and God said until the cities were ruined. But He also promised that a holy seed, a remnant, would remain, like the stump of a tree after it is cut down.""", ("Isaiah", 6)),
C("A sign for the house of David", "Isaiah told King Ahaz that a virgin would conceive and bear a son named Immanuel, “God with us.”",
"""About 734 BC, King Ahaz of Judah was terrified. Two kings from the north, Syria and Israel, were marching against him. Isaiah met Ahaz at the end of the water channel and told him to stay calm and trust God. He even invited Ahaz to ask for a sign. Ahaz refused, putting on a religious face. In fact he had already decided to ask Assyria for help.

Isaiah said, “Therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign. Behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel.” The name means “God with us.” Matthew’s Gospel applies this to the birth of Jesus. Isaiah also wrote, “For to us a child is born, to us a son is given; and the government shall be upon his shoulder, and his name shall be called Wonderful Counselor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace.”""", ("Isaiah", 7)),
C("The suffering servant", "Isaiah described a servant who would be wounded for our transgressions and bear the sin of many.",
"""In chapter 53, Isaiah describes a servant who is “despised and rejected by men; a man of sorrows and acquainted with grief.” “Surely he has borne our griefs and carried our sorrows; yet we esteemed him stricken, smitten by God, and afflicted. But he was pierced for our transgressions; he was crushed for our iniquities; upon him was the chastisement that brought us peace, and with his wounds we are healed.”

It goes on, “He was oppressed, and he was afflicted, yet he opened not his mouth; like a lamb that is led to the slaughter.” Written about 700 years before Jesus, it reads like a description of the crucifixion. When the Ethiopian official in Acts was reading this passage in his chariot, Philip started with this very text and told him the good news about Jesus.""", ("Isaiah", 53)),
C("Comfort and the future", "Isaiah also spoke words of comfort and described a future where God would make all things new.",
"""Later chapters speak to people who will be in exile, long after Isaiah’s own lifetime. They begin: “Comfort, comfort my people, says your God.” Isaiah says that grass withers and flowers fade, “but the word of our God will stand forever.” He promises that “those who wait for the Lord shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings like eagles; they shall run and not be weary; they shall walk and not faint.”

He also looks ahead to a new creation where “the wolf shall dwell with the lamb” and “they shall not hurt or destroy in all my holy mountain.” When Jesus stood up in the synagogue in Nazareth, He read from Isaiah 61, “The Spirit of the Lord God is upon me, because the Lord has anointed me to bring good news to the poor,” and said, “Today this Scripture has been fulfilled in your hearing.”

The Bible does not record Isaiah’s death. Jewish tradition says he was killed under the evil King Manasseh, but the Bible does not say.""", ("Isaiah", 40))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Isaiah’s story begins with seeing how holy God is, and then seeing how sinful he himself was. Many people in the Bible have the same experience: the closer they get to God, the more they see their need. But God’s answer was not to throw Isaiah out. It was to cleanse him and send him.

When God asks, “Whom shall I send?”, He is asking each of us in some way. Isaiah’s answer is a good one: “Here I am.”

Finally, Isaiah’s promises are for everyone. A child will be born. A servant will suffer. A new world is coming. Whatever the headlines look like, the Bible says that the word of our God will stand forever.""", ("Isaiah", 6))])

LONG["elisha"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Elisha was", """Elisha was a prophet in the northern kingdom of Israel, about 850 to 800 BC. He was the son of a farmer named Shaphat from the village of Abel Meholah. His mentor was the great prophet Elijah, and Elisha eventually took his place.

Israel was ruled by kings who worshiped Baal, and God’s prophets were often in danger. Elisha’s ministry is known for many miracles, and for the way he cared for ordinary people, widows, foreigners and soldiers.""", ("1 Kings", 19)),
("Why his story matters", """Elisha’s story shows God’s power and His care at a personal scale. It also shows that God’s kindness goes beyond Israel’s borders.""")],
cards=[
C("The cloak", "Elijah found Elisha plowing and threw his cloak over him. Elisha burned his plow and followed.",
"""One day Elijah found Elisha plowing a field with twelve pairs of oxen. That means the family was fairly wealthy, and Elisha was working with the twelfth pair. Elijah walked up and threw his cloak over him, the sign of passing on a prophet’s calling. Elisha asked to say goodbye to his parents and Elijah let him.

Then Elisha did something drastic. He took the pair of oxen, killed them, used the plow as firewood to cook the meat and gave it to the people to eat. He had destroyed his old way of life and was not planning to go back. He got up, followed Elijah, and served him.""", ("1 Kings", 19)),
C("The double portion", "When Elijah was taken up to heaven in a whirlwind, Elisha picked up his cloak and parted the Jordan.",
"""Years later, Elijah’s time was ending. Three times Elijah told Elisha to stay behind, and three times Elisha said, “As the Lord lives, and as you yourself live, I will not leave you.” At the Jordan River, Elijah struck the water with his cloak and it divided so they crossed on dry ground. Elijah asked, “Ask what I shall do for you, before I am taken from you.” Elisha said, “Please let there be a double portion of your spirit on me.”

As they walked and talked, a chariot of fire and horses of fire separated them, and Elijah went up in a whirlwind into heaven. Elisha cried out, “My father, my father! The chariots of Israel and its horsemen!” He picked up Elijah’s cloak, struck the Jordan and said, “Where is the Lord, the God of Elijah?” The water divided again, and the prophets watching said, “The spirit of Elijah rests on Elisha.”""", ("2 Kings", 2)),
C("A widow and a woman from Shunem", "Elisha multiplied a widow’s oil, and he raised the son of a Shunammite woman who had been kind to him.",
"""A widow of one of the prophets came to Elisha in desperation. Her husband had died with debts, and a creditor was about to take her two sons as slaves. “What shall I do for you? Tell me, what have you in the house?” “Your servant has nothing in the house except a jar of oil.” Elisha told her to borrow empty jars from her neighbors, as many as she could, close the door and pour. The oil kept flowing until every jar was full. She sold the oil, paid her debt and lived on the rest.

In the village of Shunem, a wealthy woman noticed Elisha passing by and built a small upstairs room for him, with a bed, table, chair and lamp. Wanting to repay her, Elisha promised her a son, though her husband was old. A year later, she had a boy. Some years later, the child got a terrible headache in the field and died in his mother’s lap. She laid him on Elisha’s bed and rode to find the prophet. Elisha stretched himself over the child, mouth to mouth, and the boy sneezed seven times and opened his eyes.""", ("2 Kings", 4)),
C("Naaman", "A foreign commander with leprosy was healed after washing seven times in the Jordan, and he declared, “There is no God in all the earth but in Israel.”",
"""Naaman was the commander of the army of Syria, a great man but with a skin disease often translated “leprosy.” His wife’s servant girl was a captive Israelite. She said, “If only my lord were with the prophet who is in Samaria! He would cure him of his leprosy.” The Syrian king sent Naaman with a huge gift of silver, gold and clothing.

Elisha did not even come out. He sent a messenger: “Go and wash in the Jordan seven times, and your flesh shall be restored.” Naaman was furious. He had expected a show. Rivers in his own country were cleaner. But his servants said, “If the prophet had commanded you to do some great thing, would you not have done it? How much more, when he says to you, ‘Wash, and be clean’?” Naaman went down, dipped seven times, and “his flesh was restored like the flesh of a little child.”

He told Elisha, “Behold, I know that there is no God in all the earth but in Israel.” Elisha refused any payment. Elisha’s servant Gehazi, however, ran after Naaman and lied to get some of the gifts. When he came back, Elisha knew. Naaman’s leprosy came on Gehazi instead.""", ("2 Kings", 5)),
C("More than those with us", "At Dothan, Elisha’s servant saw an army and was afraid. Elisha prayed, and the servant saw an army of fire around them.",
"""The king of Syria kept losing battles because Elisha told the king of Israel where the Syrians were hiding. The Syrian king sent horses and chariots and a great army to Dothan, where Elisha was staying, and surrounded the city at night.

Elisha’s servant got up early, saw the army and cried, “Alas, my master! What shall we do?” Elisha answered, “Do not be afraid, for those who are with us are more than those who are with them.” Then he prayed, “O Lord, please open his eyes that he may see.” The Lord opened the servant’s eyes, “and behold, the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha.”

Elisha then prayed that the Syrian army be struck blind. He led them to Samaria. When their eyes were opened they were in the middle of the Israelite capital. The king of Israel wanted to kill them. Elisha said, “Set food and water before them, that they may eat and drink and go to their master.” A great feast was prepared, and the Syrians went home. “And the Syrians did not come again on raids into the land of Israel.”""", ("2 Kings", 6)),
C("His last miracle", "Even after Elisha died, a dead man thrown into his tomb touched his bones and came back to life.",
"""Elisha was old and fell ill. King Joash of Israel came to him, weeping: “My father, my father! The chariots of Israel and its horsemen!” the same words Elisha had said when Elijah was taken. Elisha told the king to shoot an arrow out the window and then to strike the ground with the arrows. Joash struck only three times. Elisha was angry: five or six times would have meant complete victory over Syria. He had stopped short.

Elisha died and was buried. Not long afterward, a funeral party was burying a man when they saw a band of raiders coming. They threw the body into Elisha’s tomb and ran. “And as soon as the man touched the bones of Elisha, he revived and stood on his feet.” Even after death, God’s power was still at work.""", ("2 Kings", 13))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Elisha showed God’s power, but he also showed God’s kindness. He helped a widow with her jar of oil, a family with a lost child, and a foreign general with an illness. The same God who parts rivers takes interest in small needs.

The story of Dothan is for anyone who feels outnumbered. We often see only the army around us. Elisha prayed for his servant to see what was real. The Bible says that God’s angels are “ministering spirits sent out to serve.”

Naaman’s story also reminds us that God’s way is often simpler than we expect. Naaman wanted a grand ceremony. God asked him to wash. Trusting what God says, even when it seems too plain, is often the first step to healing.""", ("2 Kings", 6))])

LONG["job"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Job was", """Job lived in a land called Uz, and the Bible does not say exactly when. His way of life, with large herds and the father acting as priest for his family, looks like the time of Abraham or soon after. The Bible says he was “blameless and upright, one who feared God and turned away from evil.”

He was the richest man in the East. He had 7,000 sheep, 3,000 camels, 500 yoke of oxen, 500 donkeys and many servants. He also had seven sons and three daughters. Every time his children held feasts, Job offered sacrifices for each one, just in case they had sinned.""", ("Job", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """The book of Job is about suffering, and the question every person asks: why does a good person suffer? The book does not give a simple answer. It shows what faith looks like when answers are not available.""")],
cards=[
C("Everything lost in a day", "In one day Job lost his animals, servants and all ten children. He fell to the ground and worshiped.",
"""The book opens in heaven. God points to Job as an example of a faithful man, and Satan, the accuser, says that Job only serves God because of his blessings. “Stretch out your hand and touch all that he has, and he will curse you to your face.” God allowed Satan to test him, but not to touch Job himself.

On one day, messengers came one after another. Raiders had stolen the oxen and donkeys and killed the servants. Fire from heaven burned up the sheep and shepherds. Another band took the camels. And finally a great wind from the desert struck the house where Job’s ten children were eating and killed them all.

Job tore his robe, shaved his head, fell to the ground and worshiped. “Naked I came from my mother’s womb, and naked shall I return. The Lord gave, and the Lord has taken away; blessed be the name of the Lord.” The Bible says, “In all this Job did not sin or charge God with wrong.”""", ("Job", 1)),
C("Sores and silence", "Job was struck with painful sores. His wife said, “Curse God and die.” Three friends came and sat in silence for seven days.",
"""Satan said, “Skin for skin! All that a man has he will give for his life. But stretch out your hand and touch his bone and his flesh, and he will curse you to your face.” God allowed it again, but told Satan to spare Job’s life. Job was struck with painful sores from the soles of his feet to the top of his head. He sat in the ashes and scraped himself with a piece of broken pottery.

His wife, who had also lost her children, said, “Do you still hold fast your integrity? Curse God and die.” Job replied, “You speak as one of the foolish women would speak. Shall we receive good from God, and shall we not receive evil?”

Three friends, Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar, heard of his trouble and came to comfort him. When they saw him, they could hardly recognize him. They wept, tore their robes and sat on the ground with him for seven days and seven nights. No one said a word, “for they saw that his suffering was very great.” That silence was the best thing they did for him.""", ("Job", 2)),
C("The friends’ answers", "Job’s friends said he must have sinned, but Job insisted he had not and cried out for an answer.",
"""Then Job spoke, and his words were full of pain. He wished he had never been born. His friends tried to explain. Their argument was simple: God is just; God punishes sin; you are suffering; so you must have sinned. Eliphaz said, “Who that was innocent ever perished?” They urged Job to repent.

Job said they were wrong. He knew he was not perfect, but this suffering did not fit any sin of his. “Miserable comforters are you all.” Through all his complaining, he kept holding on to God. In one of the great lines of the book, he said, “For I know that my Redeemer lives, and at the last he will stand upon the earth. And after my skin has been thus destroyed, yet in my flesh I shall see God.” He wanted to bring his case before God Himself.

A younger man named Elihu then spoke, defending God’s justice. The book gives long speeches before God finally answers.""", ("Job", 19)),
C("God answers from the whirlwind", "God spoke to Job and asked him questions about creation. Job fell silent.",
"""Then “the Lord answered Job out of the whirlwind.” God did not explain why Job had suffered. Instead He asked questions: “Where were you when I laid the foundation of the earth? Tell me, if you have understanding. Who determined its measurements? ... Have you commanded the morning since your days began? ... Do you give the horse his might?”

For four chapters, God described the stars, the sea, the storm, the lion, the eagle and the great creatures Behemoth and Leviathan. The point was not to scold Job but to show him the scale of God’s wisdom and care. Job realized how little he knew. “I had heard of you by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees you; therefore I despise myself, and repent in dust and ashes.”

What changed Job was not an explanation but an encounter. He had asked for a hearing, and now he had met God.""", ("Job", 38)),
C("Restored", "God told Job’s friends they were wrong. Job prayed for them, and God gave him twice what he had before.",
"""God then turned to Eliphaz and said, “My anger burns against you and against your two friends, for you have not spoken of me what is right, as my servant Job has.” God told them to bring sacrifices, and Job would pray for them. It was a remarkable moment: Job, who had been blamed and accused, prayed for his friends.

After he prayed for them, “the Lord restored the fortunes of Job, and gave Job twice as much as he had before.” His family and friends came to eat with him and comfort him. He ended with 14,000 sheep, 6,000 camels, 1,000 yoke of oxen and 1,000 donkeys. He had seven more sons and three daughters. He named his daughters Jemimah, Keziah and Keren-happuch, and the Bible says that nowhere in all the land were there women so beautiful. He lived another 140 years and saw four generations of his family.

The New Testament remembers him: “You have heard of the steadfastness of Job, and you have seen the purpose of the Lord, how the Lord is compassionate and merciful.”""", ("Job", 42))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Job’s story tells us that suffering is not always a punishment. His friends were sure of their answers, and they were wrong. When we are tempted to explain someone else’s pain, the best thing may be what they did on the first day: sit with them, and say nothing.

It also gives us permission to be honest with God. Job complained, argued and asked. God did not reject him for it. In the end, God said Job had spoken rightly of Him, and the friends with the neat answers had not.

Finally, the book teaches that we do not need to know why in order to trust Him. Job never got an answer to his “why”. What he got was God Himself. And the Bible says the story ends well, because “the Lord is compassionate and merciful.”""", ("James", 5))])

LONG["saul"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Saul was", """Saul was the first king of Israel, around 1050 BC. He came from the tribe of Benjamin, the smallest of the twelve tribes. His father was Kish, a wealthy man. The Bible describes Saul as a handsome young man and the tallest man in Israel, “from his shoulders upward he was taller than any of the people.”

Israel had never had a king. For centuries, the tribes had been led by judges. Now the people were being attacked by the Philistines and others, and they wanted a king “like all the nations.” God told the prophet Samuel to give them one.""", ("1 Samuel", 9)),
("Why his story matters", """Saul’s story is one of the saddest in the Bible. He started well and ended in ruin. It shows how a person with every advantage can lose it all through pride, disobedience and jealousy.""")],
cards=[
C("Looking for donkeys", "Saul went to find his father’s lost donkeys and ended up being anointed king.",
"""Saul was sent by his father to look for some lost donkeys. After days of searching, his servant suggested they ask the prophet in the nearby town for help. That prophet was Samuel. The day before, God had told Samuel, “Tomorrow about this time I will send to you a man from the land of Benjamin, and you shall anoint him to be prince over my people Israel.”

When Samuel saw Saul he knew. “Behold the man of whom I spoke to you! He it is who shall restrain my people.” Saul answered with surprising humility: “Am I not a Benjaminite, from the least of the tribes of Israel? And is not my clan the humblest of all the clans of the tribe of Benjamin?” Samuel gave him a place of honor at the feast and the next morning poured oil on his head and kissed him.

When Samuel later announced him as king before all Israel, they could not find him. “He has hidden himself among the baggage.” They ran and brought him out, and he stood head and shoulders above everyone. “Long live the king!”""", ("1 Samuel", 10)),
C("A good beginning", "Saul defeated the Ammonites at Jabesh-gilead, and the people wanted to celebrate. He gave God the credit.",
"""Soon after, Nahash, king of the Ammonites, surrounded the town of Jabesh-gilead and said he would make peace only if he could put out every man’s right eye. The people of the city sent for help. When Saul heard, the Spirit of God came upon him powerfully. He cut up a pair of oxen and sent the pieces through all Israel, saying that anyone who did not follow him would be treated the same way. Over 300,000 men came. Saul divided them into three groups and attacked at dawn, and the Ammonites were routed.

The people wanted to kill those who had earlier doubted Saul as king. Saul said, “Not a man shall be put to death this day, for today the Lord has worked salvation in Israel.” At this point, Saul gave God the credit and showed mercy. It was a good start.""", ("1 Samuel", 11)),
C("Where it went wrong", "Saul offered a sacrifice himself instead of waiting for Samuel, and later spared the best of the Amalekites’ animals.",
"""Saul’s troubles began with impatience. Facing the Philistine army, Samuel had told him to wait seven days at Gilgal. When Samuel did not come on time and the soldiers began to scatter, Saul offered the burnt offering himself, which was the priest’s job. Samuel arrived right after. “You have done foolishly. You have not kept the command of the Lord your God.” Saul’s dynasty would not last.

The second failure was worse. God told Saul to completely destroy the Amalekites, an enemy who had attacked Israel when they left Egypt. Saul spared their king Agag and the best of the sheep and cattle. Samuel confronted him, and Saul said the animals were for sacrifice. Samuel’s answer: “To obey is better than sacrifice.” When Samuel turned to leave, Saul grabbed his robe and it tore. “The Lord has torn the kingdom of Israel from you this day.” Saul admitted, “I have sinned; yet honor me now before the elders of my people.” He cared more about his image than about being right with God.""", ("1 Samuel", 15)),
C("Jealousy and the spear", "Saul became jealous of David, tried to pin him to the wall with a spear, and hunted him for years.",
"""The Spirit of the Lord left Saul, and “a harmful spirit from the Lord tormented him.” His servants recommended a musician, and a young shepherd named David came to play the harp. It calmed him. Then David killed Goliath and became a hero. As the army returned, women danced and sang: “Saul has struck down his thousands, and David his ten thousands.” “And Saul eyed David from that day on.”

The next day, while David played for him, Saul threw his spear, trying to pin David to the wall. David escaped twice. Saul sent David on dangerous missions, hoping the Philistines would kill him. He even tried to have his own son Jonathan, David’s best friend, put to death for helping David. For years Saul hunted David through the wilderness with 3,000 soldiers. Twice David had the chance to kill him, once in a cave at En Gedi and once while Saul slept in his camp, and each time David spared him: “I will not put out my hand against the Lord’s anointed.” Saul wept and said, “You are more righteous than I.” But it did not last.""", ("1 Samuel", 18)),
C("The last night", "Saul visited a medium at Endor the night before the final battle. The next day he and his sons were killed at Mount Gilboa.",
"""By the end, Saul was desperate. The Philistine army gathered at Shunem. “When Saul saw the army of the Philistines, he was afraid, and his heart trembled greatly.” He had thrown out mediums and necromancers from the land, which was right, but now God was not answering him by dreams or prophets. In the night, in disguise, he went to a medium in Endor and asked her to bring up Samuel, who had died. The Bible says Samuel appeared with a hard message: “Tomorrow you and your sons shall be with me.”

The next day, on Mount Gilboa, the Philistines overran Israel. Saul’s three sons, including Jonathan, were killed. Saul was wounded by archers. Rather than be captured and abused, he fell on his own sword. The Philistines hung his body on the wall of Beth-shan. Brave men from Jabesh-gilead, the town Saul had rescued at the start of his reign, crossed the Jordan at night, took the bodies down and buried them.

When David heard, he sang a lament: “How the mighty have fallen in the midst of the battle! Saul and Jonathan, beloved and lovely! In life and in death they were not divided.” He mourned the king who had tried to kill him.""", ("1 Samuel", 31))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Saul had height, looks, a throne and a good start. What he lacked was a heart that would obey God when it cost him something. He wanted to look good more than to be good. His story shows that how you begin matters less than how you finish.

Jealousy poisoned him. Instead of being thankful for David’s success, he saw it as a threat, and it ruined his mind and his home. The Bible warns that envy “makes the bones rot.” Celebrating others’ victories is a spiritual discipline.

Samuel’s words are a useful test for our own lives: “To obey is better than sacrifice.” God does not want our religious show. He wants our trust and obedience. And Saul’s story is also a reminder that God keeps His promises: He had a plan for Israel, and He raised up David to carry it on.""", ("1 Samuel", 15))])
